\section{The $D_5$ cell realized inside the group}

Let $D=\langle r,s\mid r^5=s^2=1,\ srs^{-1}=r^{-1}\rangle$ act on the vertices of a pentagon, identified with the five roots.

\paragraph{The type observable.} Set $T(r^i)=[1^5]$ if $i\equiv0$ and $[5]$ otherwise, and $T(sr^i)=[1,2,2]$. This is the cycle type of the corresponding permutation: a nontrivial rotation is a $5$-cycle; a reflection of a pentagon fixes one vertex and swaps the remaining four in two transpositions. The observable is intrinsic, since the three types are exactly the three element orders,
\[
T(g)=[1^5]\iff g=1,\qquad T(g)=[5]\iff\ord(g)=5,\qquad T(g)=[1,2,2]\iff\ord(g)=2,
\]
and it is a class function, $T(hgh^{-1})=T(g)$, as any Frobenius type must be.

\paragraph{The coset observable.} Set $C(r^i)=0$ and $C(sr^i)=1$. This is a surjective homomorphism onto $C_2$.

\begin{theorem}[$D_5^{\ab}\cong C_2$]\label{thm:d5ab}
The commutator subgroup $[D,D]$ is exactly the rotation subgroup $\ker C$; hence $C$ is the abelianization map.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The quotient by the rotation subgroup is the abelian group $C_2$, so every commutator lies in the kernel. Conversely every rotation is a \emph{single} commutator: $[s,r^{m}]=s^{-1}r^{-m}sr^{m}=r^{2m}$, and $2$ is invertible modulo $5$, so as $m$ ranges over $\mathbb{Z}/5$ so does $2m$; thus each $r^k$ is a commutator and the rotation subgroup lies in $[D,D]$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Determination and the cell]
$T(g)=T(g')$ implies $C(g)=C(g')$, since $[1^5]$ and $[5]$ occur only for rotations and $[1,2,2]$ only for reflections. Hence $I(T;C)=\log_2|C_2|=1$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:closed}.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Cell counts]
The nonzero cells have counts $1$ (type $[1^5]$, coset $0$), $4$ (type $[5]$, coset $0$) and $5$ (type $[1,2,2]$, coset $1$); dividing by $|D|=10$ reproduces exactly the abstract $D_5$ table, so the group's own $H(T)=\tfrac15+\tfrac L2$, $I(T;C)=1$ and $H(T\mid C)=\tfrac L2-\tfrac45$.
\end{theorem}

\section{Locating the quadratic character: $\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{-5})$}

For a generic quintic the surjection $G\to C_2$ is the sign character and the corresponding quadratic field is $\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{\disc f})$, so the transmitted bit is $\left(\frac{\disc f}{p}\right)$. But $D_5\subseteq A_5$: rotations are $5$-cycles and reflections are products of two transpositions, so every element is even, $\disc f$ is a perfect square, and the discriminant character is trivial. The $C_2$ must therefore live elsewhere: in the \emph{quadratic resolvent}, the unique quadratic subfield $K$ of the degree-$10$ splitting field, fixed by $[D_5,D_5]=C_5$.

Theory confines $K$ to a finite list: since $K$ lies in the splitting field, which is ramified exactly at the primes dividing $\disc(f)$, we have $K=\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt d)$ for a squarefree $d$ built from that prime set. Enumerating the candidates and comparing, prime by prime, the Kronecker symbol $\left(\frac{d}{p}\right)$ with the observed coset (type in $\{[1^5],[5]\}$ versus $[1,2,2]$) singles out one candidate. For $f(x)=x^5+20x+32$ the unique match, at perfect agreement, is
\[
K=\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{-5}),\qquad \disc(K)=-20,\qquad m^\ast=20,
\]
with split classes $p\equiv1,3,7,9\pmod{20}$ and inert classes $p\equiv11,13,17,19\pmod{20}$. Explicitly: \emph{$x^5+20x+32$ factors modulo $p$ with two quadratic factors exactly when $-5$ is a non-residue modulo $p$.}

Two remarks. First, the search that produced the polynomial is elementary: among trinomials $x^5+ax+b$ with $|a|,|b|\le60$ one keeps the irreducible ones with square discriminant (forcing $G\subseteq A_5$) and then reads the type histogram, which distinguishes $D_5$ from $C_5$ and $A_5$; four such $D_5$ trinomials appear inside that box. Second, this is why the histogram \emph{is} the group readout: in degree five the rate vector $(1/10,4/10,1/2)$ on types $\{[1^5],[5],[1,2,2]\}$ occurs for $D_5$ alone.

\section{Algorithms and numerical corroboration}

\paragraph{Factorization type modulo $p$.} Use distinct-degree factorization: set $h_0=x$, $F=f$; for $i\ge1$, put $h_i=h_{i-1}^{\,p}\bmod F$ and $g_i=\gcd(F,h_i-x)$, the product of the irreducible factors of $F$ of degree exactly $i$; record $\deg g_i/i$ factors of degree $i$ and replace $F$ by $F/g_i$. For fixed small $n$ the cost is $O(n^3\log p)$ field operations.

\paragraph{Exact Shannon quantities.} For a table of rational cells, compute all entropies by the definitions and compare with the $\mathbb{Q}$-linear closed forms in $1,\log_2 3,\log_2 5$; cost $O(|\iota||\kappa|)$.

\paragraph{Empirical channel.} Over a range of primes, form the empirical table of (type of $f\bmod p$, residue $p\bmod m^\ast$) and compute its Shannon quantities; by Theorem~\ref{thm:dial} the limit equals the coset-level value, with deviations governed by the Chebotarev error term.

\paragraph{Observed values.} For $x^5+20x+32$ over primes up to $10^6$: the type rates match $\{1/10,4/10,1/2\}$ to within $0.002$, no type outside the $D_5$ menu ever occurs, $H(T)=1.3610$, and $I(p\bmod 20;T)=1.0000$ against the law's $1.0000$. The agreement between the type parity and $\left(\frac{-20}{p}\right)$ is exact. A simulation of the semiprime channel returns pair information $1.0000$ and which-factor wall $0.0000$, matching Theorems~\ref{thm:multi} and~\ref{thm:wall}.

\section{Discussion}

\paragraph{What the channel measures.} The quantity $I(T;C)$ answers a question older than information theory: which features of the splitting behaviour of a polynomial are governed by congruences? Class field theory answers ``exactly the abelian ones''; the channel makes that quantitative. $H(T)$ is the total randomness of the factorization, $I(T;C)$ the part reciprocity reaches, $H(T\mid C)$ the part it cannot, and the gap identity says they add up with nothing left over. The extremes are instructive: $C_5$ has gap zero (all congruence) while $A_5$ has information zero (all non-abelian).

\paragraph{Why exactness is the point.} Three theorems conspire. The Abelianization Law makes $I$ the logarithm of an integer rather than an integral of a density; the residue-dial invariance shows that substituting the observable residue for the idealized coset does not perturb $I$ at all; and the Saturation Criterion certifies that any shortfall is caused by an identifiable ambiguous type, as with the half-bit deficit of $F_{20}$.

\paragraph{Limitations.} The framework is exact about densities; finite prime scans see the Chebotarev error term, so empirical agreement should be read as consistency rather than proof. The identification of the resolvent for $x^5+20x+32$ is a theory-constrained search plus verification, not a closed-form derivation. Finally the results concern the cycle-type observable; finer observables (the Frobenius class itself) give strictly larger channels, differing in degree five only for $A_5$ and $F_{20}$, where distinct classes share a cycle type.

\section{Future work}

\begin{conjecture}[Rank formula]
For any finite group $G$ with abelianization $A$, the uniform type/coset channel satisfies
$I(T;C)=\log_2|A|-\sum_t p(t)\log_2 k_t$, where $k_t$ is the number of distinct cosets hit by elements of type $t$.
\end{conjecture}
The Saturation Criterion is the case where all $k_t=1$. Each conjugacy class has a single image in $A$, so all ambiguity comes from distinct classes sharing a cycle type: a purely combinatorial collision. $F_{20}$ is the smallest worked example, with $k_{[4]}=2$ and deficit $\tfrac12$.

\begin{conjecture}[Deficit quantization]
For every transitive $G\le S_n$ the deficit $\log_2|A|-I(T;C)$ is a dyadic rational, and in particular is $0$ or at least $1/|A|$.
\end{conjecture}
In degree at most five the observed deficits are $0$ (four cells) and $\tfrac12$ ($F_{20}$). Since the deficit is $\sum_t p(t)\log_2 k_t$ with $p(t)\in\frac1{|G|}\mathbb{Z}$, it is rational exactly when each $k_t$ is a power of two; a sextic group with a type straddling three cosets of $C_6$ would force an irrational deficit and refute the quantization.

\begin{conjecture}[Multi-prime saturation and the wall hierarchy]
For $k$ independent Frobenius classes with observables $(T_1,\dots,T_k)$ and product coset, $I=\log_2|A|$ for every $k\ge1$ whenever the type determines the coset, while the information about \emph{any} proper sub-product of the cosets is exactly $0$.
\end{conjecture}
The extreme cases are Theorems~\ref{thm:multi} and~\ref{thm:wall}; the general sub-product wall remains open. The mechanism is that a product of $k$ independent uniform elements of an abelian group is uniform and independent of any $k-1$ of them.

Beyond these: the sixteen transitive groups of degree six as a direct test of the rank formula; a non-abelian analogue replacing the coset by a higher-dimensional character value; effective Chebotarev error terms giving honest confidence intervals for empirical cells; and a converse to the wall classifying the zero-information pairs of observables on $G^k$.

\section{Conclusion}

The transitive quintic row is complete. For $C_5$, $D_5$, $F_{20}$, $A_5$, $S_5$ the information that the factorization shape of a quintic modulo $p$ carries about the residue class of $p$ is exactly $\log_2 5-\tfrac85$, $1$, $\tfrac32$, $0$, $1$ bits, with residual entropies $0$, $\tfrac12\log_25-\tfrac45$, $\tfrac14\log_25-\tfrac25$, $\tfrac2{15}+\tfrac7{20}\log_23+\tfrac5{12}\log_25$ and $\tfrac25+\tfrac5{24}\log_25+\tfrac{17}{40}\log_23$. One law governs all five: when the cycle type determines the abelianization coset the channel transmits exactly $\log_2|G^{\ab}|$ bits and the gap is exactly the coset-conditioned type entropy. The law now has a proved converse, so the one cell that falls short does so for an identified reason; the residue dial is proved information-neutral; and the semiprime and $k$-prime channels transmit the same $\log_2|A|$ bits behind a hard zero wall.

\end{document}

